---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'cli', label: 'The a1 CLI' },
   { id: 'problem', label: 'The gap' },
   { id: 'output', label: 'What a session returns' },
   { id: 'next', label: 'Next' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Test accessibility from the terminal"
   summary="a11ied is a command line tool, an MCP server, and a TypeScript library for accessibility testing. All three run axe against a page and drive VoiceOver, NVDA, or a simulated screen reader, then report both against WCAG."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <div class="feature-block">
      <div class="feature-grid">
         <section class="feature-card">
            <h2>Command line tool</h2>
            <div class="feature-demo">
               <code>npm install --global a11ied</code>
            </div>
            <p>
               The <code>a1</code> binary runs scans, screen reader sessions, and WCAG lookups
               from a terminal or a CI job.
            </p>
            <p class="feature-link"><a href="/reference/cli">CLI reference</a></p>
         </section>

         <section class="feature-card">
            <h2>MCP server</h2>
            <div class="feature-demo">
               <code>a1 mcp</code>
            </div>
            <p>
               The same checks as MCP tools, so a coding agent can test the page it just
               changed.
            </p>
            <p class="feature-link"><a href="/reference/mcp">MCP tools</a></p>
         </section>

         <section class="feature-card">
            <h2>TypeScript library</h2>
            <div class="feature-demo">
               <code>import {'{'} screenReader {'}'} from 'a11ied/test'</code>
            </div>
            <p>
               Screen reader tests in Vitest or any other runner, with matchers for what the
               reader announced.
            </p>
            <p class="feature-link"><a href="/reference/api">TypeScript API</a></p>
         </section>
      </div>
      <p>All three call the same runtime, so a result means the same thing in each.</p>
   </div>

   <section class="feature-block" id="cli">
      <h2>What can you do with the a1 CLI?</h2>
      <div class="feature-grid feature-grid--wide">
         <section class="feature-card">
            <h3>Scan a page</h3>
            <div class="feature-demo">
               <code>a1 audit /checkout</code>
               <samp>&#10007; 2 problems to fix.</samp>
               <samp>1.1.1  Non-text Content  [A]</samp>
               <samp>4.1.2  Name, Role, Value  [A]</samp>
            </div>
            <p>
               axe-core checks the rendered page for the failures a machine can see. Each
               failure comes back under the WCAG requirement it breaks, with the elements to
               fix.
            </p>
         </section>

         <section class="feature-card">
            <h3>Print the accessibility tree</h3>
            <div class="feature-demo">
               <code>a1 tree /checkout</code>
               <samp>- main:</samp>
               <samp>  - heading "Checkout" [level=1]</samp>
               <samp>  - heading "Order summary" [level=2]</samp>
            </div>
            <p>
               The roles, names, and levels a browser hands to assistive technology, before any
               screen reader is involved.
            </p>
         </section>

         <section class="feature-card">
            <h3>List the form fields</h3>
            <div class="feature-demo">
               <code>a1 sr elements form-field</code>
               <samp>1. textbox: Email  textbox, Email, required</samp>
               <samp>2. textbox: Card number  textbox, Card number, invalid</samp>
            </div>
            <p>
               Fields, headings, links, landmarks, or tables, each with the state a screen
               reader announces for it. The card number here is announced as invalid.
            </p>
         </section>

         <section class="feature-card">
            <h3>Tab through the page</h3>
            <div class="feature-demo">
               <code>a1 sr press Tab --phrase</code>
               <samp>textbox, Email, required</samp>
            </div>
            <p>
               Press the keys a person would press, and get back the one phrase the reader
               announced. Repeat it and the focus order becomes a list you can read.
            </p>
         </section>

         <section class="feature-card">
            <h3>Fail the build</h3>
            <div class="feature-demo">
               <code>a1 sr expect "button, Help"</code>
               <samp>Matched:   nothing</samp>
               <samp>Result:    fail</samp>
            </div>
            <p>
               The transcript is text, so a test can require a phrase. The command exits 4 when
               the phrase never appears, which stops a CI run.
            </p>
         </section>

         <section class="feature-card">
            <h3>Look up a requirement</h3>
            <div class="feature-demo">
               <code>a1 wcag rule button-name</code>
               <samp>Criteria (1)</samp>
               <samp>  4.1.2  Name, Role, Value  [A]</samp>
            </div>
            <p>
               All of WCAG 2.2 offline. Map an axe rule to what it breaks, search the criteria,
               or read one in full with the W3C guidance.
            </p>
         </section>
      </div>
      <p>
         There is more than this. <code>a1 doctor</code> checks the machine, <code>a1 sr
         batch</code> runs a scripted flow over one session, <code>a1 sr transcript</code>
         prints everything said so far, and <code>a1 sr screenshot</code> captures what the
         VoiceOver cursor is on. The <a href="/reference/cli">CLI reference</a> lists every
         command, and <code>a1 --help</code> prints them in the terminal.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="ledger" id="problem">
      <h2>The gap</h2>
      <p>
         Accessibility work is measured against the <a
            href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/">Web Content Accessibility
         Guidelines (WCAG)</a>, a published standard of numbered requirements called success
         criteria.
      </p>
      <p>
         <a href="https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core">axe-core</a>, the engine behind most
         accessibility scans, reads the rendered HTML and reports what it finds there. That
         settles part of the standard.
      </p>
      <p>
         The rest need a person. Whether a form announces its error, whether focus lands
         somewhere sensible, whether a dialog traps the reader: none of that is visible in the
         HTML. Answering it means opening <a
            href="https://support.apple.com/guide/voiceover/welcome/mac">VoiceOver</a> or <a
            href="https://www.nvaccess.org/">NVDA</a> and listening, which is slow enough that
         most teams skip it.
      </p>
      <p>
         a11ied scripts that half. It starts a screen reader, moves through a page, and returns
         what was said as text you can read, diff, and assert on.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="ledger" id="output">
      <h2>What a session returns</h2>
      <pre><code>a1 sr walk https://example.com/checkout</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"spokenPhraseLog": [
  "document",
  "heading, Checkout, level 1",
  "form",
  "Email",
  "textbox, Email, required",
  "Card number",
  "textbox, Card number",
  "button, Pay now",
  "button",
  "end of form",
  "end of document"
]</code></pre>
      <p>
         That is the order a screen reader user hears the page in. The email field announces its
         required state and the card field does not, and the last button has no name at all, so
         it is announced as "button" and nobody can tell what it does.
      </p>
      <p>
         Because it is text, a test can assert on it. The <a href="/guides/testing">testing
         guide</a> covers writing those assertions.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="next">
      <h2>Next</h2>
      <p>
         The <a href="/quickstart">quickstart</a> installs a11ied and takes one page from an axe
         run to a screen reader transcript. It runs on the simulated screen reader, so it needs
         nothing else installed.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
